  { question: 'How long to marinate beef jerky?', answer: 'Marinate beef jerky for 8–24 hours in the refrigerator for best results. A minimum of 6 hours delivers acceptable flavor. 12–16 hours is the sweet spot for most marinades. Beyond 24 hours, the meat texture can become mushy — especially with acidic marinades containing vinegar or citrus juice.' },
  { question: 'What is beef jerky cure and do I need it?', answer: 'Beef jerky cure is Prague Powder No. 1 (sodium nitrite mixed with salt), added at 1/4 teaspoon per 5 lbs of meat. It is not required for safe jerky when meat is properly dehydrated at 160°F, but it significantly extends shelf life, prevents gray coloring, and adds a subtle flavor depth. It is highly recommended for anyone making large batches.' },
  { question: 'Can you marinate beef jerky too long?', answer: 'Yes — marinating beyond 24 hours can make jerky mushy, especially with acidic marinades (vinegar, citrus, pineapple juice). The acids break down muscle proteins over time. Soy sauce-based marinades are more forgiving — 24–36 hours is usually safe. For the best texture, stick to 8–16 hours.' },
